---
.title = "Command line arguments",
.date = "2026-09-02",
.author = "muhammadSwa",
.layout = "section.shtml",
---

Command line arguments live in `init.minimal.args`, which exposes three views over `argv`. Pick whichever fits how you consume the arguments.

## 1. Read arguments as a slice

[`std.process.Args.toSlice`] copies all arguments into memory you own: `args[0]` is the program name, `args[1..]` are the user arguments. Use this when you need random access or want to keep the arguments around.

[]($code.siteAsset('src/13-02-01.zig').language('zig'))

## 2. Iterate arguments lazily

Read arguments one at a time with [`std.process.Args.iterate`]. Each `next()` yields a `?[:0]const u8` (sentinel-terminated slice) or `null` at the end; call `skip()` to drop the program name and `deinit()` when done.

[]($code.siteAsset('src/13-02-02.zig').language('zig'))

## 3. Access the raw argument vector

Get the raw vector with [`std.process.Args.Vector`]; the same `char **argv` a C program sees. On POSIX-like targets entries are null-terminated `[*:0]const u8` pointers; no allocation or copying is involved — convert each entry with [`std.mem.span`] (on Windows the vector is WTF-16 and on WASI without libc it is `void`; use `iterate` for portable code).

[]($code.siteAsset('src/13-02-03.zig').language('zig'))

[`std.process.Args.toSlice`]: https://ziglang.org/documentation/0.16.0/std/#std.process.Args.toSlice
[`std.process.Args.Vector`]: https://ziglang.org/documentation/0.16.0/std/#std.process.Args.Vector
[`std.process.Args.iterate`]: https://ziglang.org/documentation/0.16.0/std/#std.process.Args.iterate
[`std.mem.span`]: https://ziglang.org/documentation/0.16.0/std/#std.mem.span
